1
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.633
In this lesson, we'll go through
the basic concepts

2
00:00:04.633 --> 00:00:08.900
behind agentic document workflows,
which include RAG agents and workflows.

3
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.033
You will learn how RAG helps
answer questions about your data,

4
00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:14.500
how workflows
prescribe data flows to agents,

5
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.500
and how event-driven document
processing enhances RAG.

6
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.333
Let's dive in.

7
00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:22.100
What I want to tell you about today
are agentic document

8
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.000
workflows.

9
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.700
You're going to build a simple one today.

10
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.800
These are a new paradigm
that we've introduced

11
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.400
for applications built on top of LLMs,
like ChatGPT based on RAG,

12
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.500
but addressing the limitations of RAG

13
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.800
and moving beyond them with
the application of agentic strategies.

14
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.200
But that's a lot of unfamiliar words
all at once.

15
00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:44.233
What is RAG? What is an agentic strategy?

16
00:00:44.233 --> 00:00:47.400
Before you get started,
let's define what those things are.

17
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.600
RAG stands for Retrieval Augmented
Generation.

18
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.700
RAG is a response
to a fundamental limitation of LLMs.

19
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.133
They are trained on mountains of data,
but they aren't trained on your data.

20
00:00:57.433 --> 00:01:01.600
And usually when you're solving a problem,
you don't just need general knowledge,

21
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.400
you need to process your private data.
To get an LLM to answer questions

22
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.600
about your data,

23
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.433
you have to give the LLM your data,
but that runs into

24
00:01:09.433 --> 00:01:12.800
another fundamental limitation of our LLMs,
the context window.

25
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.300
You can only give an LLM so much data
at one time.

26
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.033
Even the most capable LLMs
can only handle about a million tokens

27
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:21.633
of information at one time.

28
00:01:21.633 --> 00:01:25.200
And you have way more data than that
in your organization, probably tens

29
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.433
or hundreds of millions.

30
00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:29.600
So you have to be selective
about what data you give an LLM.

31
00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:31.600
That exposes a challenge.

32
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.133
How do you select the data?
You want to give your LLM the most relevant

33
00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:36.033
possible data.

34
00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:39.533
It turns out the same technology
that produced LLMs, produced

35
00:01:39.533 --> 00:01:43.100
things called embedding models, which are
part of the solution to that problem.

36
00:01:43.533 --> 00:01:47.500
Embedding models take strings of data
and encode them into arrays of numbers

37
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:48.933
called vectors.

38
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:52.333
The total set of possible
vectors is known as vector space.

39
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.033
These vectors encode the meaning
of the data that you've encoded.

40
00:01:56.533 --> 00:01:59.300
You can
then store these vectors in a database.

41
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.133
Now, if you have a question
about your data,

42
00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:05.100
you can run your question
through the same embedding model.

43
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.600
And your question will also be encoded
as a vector.

44
00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.333
The magic of embedding models is that
because they encode meaning, your question,

45
00:02:12.333 --> 00:02:16.200
and the data that answers your question,
they're about similar things.

46
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.700
They mean similar things.

47
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.233
So they end up mathematically nearby
to each other in vector space.

48
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.800
You can use your database
to search for nearby vectors.

49
00:02:25.033 --> 00:02:29.000
And this is how you find the relevant data
to give to an LLM when you have a query.

50
00:02:29.433 --> 00:02:32.900
You can then take your query
and the relevant data called the context,

51
00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.900
and give both of those to another
LLM and ask it to answer the question

52
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.300
using the context.

53
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.433
We call this step generation
because it's generating an answer,

54
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.600
and the step where we search for
relevant data retrieval.

55
00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:45.633
Hence Retrieval

56
00:02:45.633 --> 00:02:48.400
Augmented Generation or RAG.

57
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.100
RAG is an incredibly powerful technique,
but it does have some limitations.

58
00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.500
One of them is complex or multi-part
questions.

59
00:02:55.733 --> 00:02:58.833
This limitation makes sense
because RAG is based on search.

60
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.133
If you have a question with lots of parts,
RAG is going to search your embeddings

61
00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:06.900
for lots of things at once, and so
the results you get will be less focused.

62
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:08.500
You'll get a ton of results,

63
00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:11.500
but they might not contain
all the information you need.

64
00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.300
There's a solution to this problem,

65
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.633
which is to split up your complex question
into a lot of simpler questions.

66
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.000
Each of those simpler

67
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.933
questions will get a much more focused
and comprehensive set of search results.

68
00:03:22.033 --> 00:03:22.100
This is a great task for our LLMs
because they are good at looking

69
00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.833
This is a great task for our LLMs
because they are good at looking

70
00:03:24.833 --> 00:03:28.500
at a complicated question and splitting it
up into smaller questions,

71
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.833
and at the other end,
they can take a bunch of answers

72
00:03:31.833 --> 00:03:35.600
to simple questions and synthesize them
into a single, coherent answer.

73
00:03:35.933 --> 00:03:38.033
This is what you're going to be doing
today,

74
00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:41.033
and the way you're going to do
it, is by building an agent.

75
00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.833
I mentioned agents and agentic
strategies earlier.

76
00:03:43.833 --> 00:03:45.233
So what is an agent?

77
00:03:45.233 --> 00:03:48.500
Well,
it's a pretty fuzzy term at LlamaIndex,

78
00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:52.400
when we say an agent, what we mean
is a piece of semi-autonomous software

79
00:03:52.533 --> 00:03:56.700
which can be given tools and a goal
and will work out how to solve the problem

80
00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.900
without needing to be given explicit step
by step instructions

81
00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.233
on how to achieve that goal.

82
00:04:01.233 --> 00:04:03.500
This is very different
from traditional programing,

83
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:06.933
where every step is
precisely defined. In LlamaIndex,

84
00:04:06.933 --> 00:04:09.933
the way that you build an agent
is by using workflows.

85
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:13.500
Workflows are the building blocks
of agentic systems in LlamaIndex.

86
00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:17.500
They are an event-based system
that lets you define a series of steps

87
00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:20.833
connected by events and pass information
from step to step.

88
00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:25.100
As you'll see, you can create
quite complex workflows with branches,

89
00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.500
loops, and parallel execution
to achieve any task that you need.

90
00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:32.400
Workflows provide your agent
with structure as fine grained or as loose

91
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:33.800
as you need it to be.

92
00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:37.500
Some agent frameworks have no structure
at all, which can lead to chaotic results.

93
00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:41.100
Others go with a graph-based approach
which makes looping and other structures

94
00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:41.933
more difficult.

95
00:04:41.933 --> 00:04:44.933
I think workflows provides
the best of both worlds.

96
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:48.333
That brings us to agentic document
workflows or ADW.

97
00:04:49.033 --> 00:04:53.733
We've already discussed the basics, RAG
workflows, and agents. Agentic document

98
00:04:53.733 --> 00:04:57.500
workflows are a way of building software
that solves real business problems

99
00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:59.833
by applying agent workflows to them

100
00:04:59.833 --> 00:05:02.400
and building them into larger
pieces of software.

101
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.000
ADW builds on top of the Power of RAG.

102
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:08.200
But unlike RAG,
which is mostly about simple questions,

103
00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.900
agentic document workflows tackle
complex problems and results

104
00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:15.600
in structured specific output
and not just plain English answers.

105
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.800
Now that we've covered the basics, in the
next lesson, we'll get started building.

